  "question": "What did ships carry from Muziris?",
  "question_lang": null,
  "answer": "According to the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea, ships from Muziris carried \"pepper, which is produced in quantity in only one region near these markets, a district called Cottonara\" [S1]. The text also states that \"there are exported great quantities of fine pearls, ivory, silk cloth, spikenard from the Ganges, malabathrum from the places in the interior, transparent stones of all kinds, diamonds and sapphires, and tortoise-shell; that from Chryse Island, and that taken among the islands along the coast of Damirica\" [S1].\n\nThe Muziris Papyrus, a cargo contract from the mid-2nd century CE, documents specific exports including \"Gangetic nard: 60 boxes\" and \"Ivory: first grade, sound\" [S6]. A Tamil literary text from the Sangam period describes Muziris as \"arriving with gold and departing with pepper\" [S5].",
